[2026-07-01T09:45:47.677565] [DEBUG] Parsed tool call: Final Answer params={'content': "twin, here's how the move and destroy tools work rn, no cap:\n\n🕹️ **move tool**  \n- when u press trigger, a raycast shoots from `rayOrigin` forward.  \n- if it hits something (a `collider`), that exact GameObject gets parented to the `rayOrigin` (ur hand/controller).  \n- it follows ur hand around in world‑space while held.  \n- when u release the trigger, the object unparents and stays where u left it.  \n\n💥 **destroy tool**  \n- same raycast deal — trigger press, raycast forward, if it hits a collider, that GameObject gets `Destroy()`'d instantly.  \n\n🟢 **do u need colliders on the trees?**  \n- **yes, 100%.** both tools (and even `SinglePlace`/`BrushPlace`) use `Physics.Raycast` to detect objects. no collider = ray goes right through 'em, so they're invisible to the tools.  \n- also make sure the colliders are **not marked as triggers** (or that `Physics.queriesHitTriggers` is enabled), bc the default raycast ignores triggers. i’d keep 'em as regular non‑trigger colliders for simplicity.  \n\n⚠️ **small heads‑up:**  \n- the move tool grabs the **exact hit.collider.gameObject**. if your tree has child colliders, grabbing a child might unparent it from the rest of the tree. best to have the main collider on the root GameObject so the whole prefab moves together.  \n- it also skips itself (`obj != gameObject`) and the root of its own transform tree to avoid grabbing the spawner or indicators, so ur safe there.  \n\nso ye, slap a `BoxCollider` or `MeshCollider` on each tree prefab, and u good to go, broski. hmu if u need a quick script to auto‑add colliders or anything else, fr 🫡🌳✨"}
